=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_dist_2_1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** OSPF area assignment on `switch_dist_2_1`'s uplink interface (eth0, 172.16.0.9/31 toward `router_core_2`).

**Mechanism:** OSPF Hello packets carry the area ID of the sending interface. Both ends of a point-to-point link must declare the *same* area for an adjacency to form. `router_core_2` advertises its eth2 (172.16.0.8/31) under area **0.0.0.1**. `switch_dist_2_1` advertises its eth0 (172.16.0.9/31) — the other half of the same /31 — under area **0.0.0.0**. Because the area IDs in the Hello packets never agree, the OSPF state machine never advances past Init; the adjacency never reaches Full. With no OSPF neighbor, `switch_dist_2_1` cannot originate or receive Type-1 LSAs for the 10.2.1.0/24 prefix, leaving it with only 3 locally-known routes. Every other router therefore has no route to 10.2.1.0/24, causing the "Destination Net Unreachable" ICMP errors seen from all remote sources. Pod 1 and the server farm are unaffected because `switch_dist_1_1`↔`router_core_1` correctly share area 0.0.0.1 on their uplink.

**Localization:** `switch_dist_2_1`, network statement for `172.16.0.8/31` in FRR's OSPF configuration (ospfd).

**Root cause:** After the change window, `switch_dist_2_1`'s uplink network was placed in area 0.0.0.0 instead of area 0.0.0.1, creating an area mismatch with `router_core_2`'s matching interface.

**Fix:** In `switch_dist_2_1`'s FRR configuration, change `network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0.0.0.0` to `network 172.16.0.8/31 area 0.0.0.1` to match `router_core_2`, then reload ospfd so the Hello packets align and the adjacency re-forms.
=== END ===